<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wenceslaus III Adam, Duke of Cieszyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1524">1524</a>–<a href="/years/1579">1579</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wenceslaus III Adam was a Piast Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a> who succeeded as a child in 1528 and ruled in person from 1545. He introduced the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> into the duchy and issued a church ordinance for it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> remained strong in Cieszyn Silesia despite the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> pressed after the Piast line ended in 1653.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iii-adam-duke-of-cieszyn.jpg" alt="Wenceslaus III Adam, Duke of Cieszyn" width="269" height="358" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jakob Seisenegger (?) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waclaw_Adam_face.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wenceslaus III Adam was the second and only surviving son of Wenceslaus II, co-Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>, and of Anna, a daughter of Frederick I, Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>, and he was born after his father's death. His grandfather, Duke Casimir II, raised him as his only heir and on his death in 1528 left the duchy to him under the regency of his mother and the Bohemian magnate Jan of Pernštejn, called the Rich. The young duke was educated at the imperial court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> but adopted the Evangelical faith. Although he counted as an adult from his mother's death in 1539, Jan of Pernštejn kept the government until 9 May 1545, giving up the regency only in return for Místek and Friedland as a pledge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Under Wenceslaus the economy of the duchy stabilised and grew, though the lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a> won by Casimir II and the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opava">Opava</a> had been lost during the regency. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> took real effect from about 1540: the Franciscan and Dominican convents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a> and later the Benedictine house at Orlová were closed, and part of their property went to the town hospital, where the duke, trained in medicine, cared for the poor, notably during the epidemic of 1570. On 24 June 1573 he issued the Land Ordinance of the Duchy of Cieszyn, a body of rules for all its inhabitants.</p><p>Despite his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> he served the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> faithfully, attending the coronation of Maximilian II as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a> in 1563 and the funeral of Ferdinand I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1565. In 1573 he built fortifications near Mosty u Jablunkova against a possible Turkish attack and stood without success for the Polish throne. The debts of his eldest son, Frederick Casimir, forced him to sell Fryštát, Skoczów and Bielsko to other Piast dukes after the son's death in 1571.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died of apoplexy after a long illness on 4 November 1579 and was buried in the former Dominican church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Cieszyn</a>. He married first Maria of Pernštejn, his guardian's daughter, in 1540, and secondly Sidonia Catharina of Saxe-Lauenburg in 1567; both wives supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> in the duchy. Of the nine children of the two marriages, the only son to outlive him was Adam Wenceslaus, born in 1574.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wenceslaus III Adam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Wenceslaus III Adam</b> introduced Lutheranism in the 16th century, and the duchy kept a strong Protestant community even after the Counter-Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cieszyn">Duchy of Cieszyn</a></li></ul></section>
